<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vertumnus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vertumnus was a god of the changing seasons, gardens and fruit, of Etruscan origin and often identified with Voltumna, chief god of the Etruscan league, whose sanctuary lay near Volsinii. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> captured Volsinii in 264 BCE his cult was brought to Rome, where a statue of him stood in the Vicus Tuscus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> tells of his wooing of the nymph Pomona.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vertumnus.jpg" alt="Vertumnus" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Vertumne_et_Pomone_-_Peter_Paul_Rubens_(1617-1619).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Roman religion Vertumnus, also written Vortumnus or Vertimnus, was the god of seasons, change and plant growth, together with gardens and fruit trees. His name most likely derives from the Etruscan Voltumna, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form was probably shaped by the verb vertere, meaning to change. Ancient writers explained the name by sound resemblances, which show how his function was understood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, writing on the Roman calendar, recalls a time when the Forum was still a reedy swamp and the god was not yet called Vertumnus from damming back the river.</p><p>Varro was convinced that Vortumnus was Etruscan and a major god, and Propertius, the main literary source for the god, also says he came from Volsinii. A temple to him stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aventine-hill">Aventine Hill</a> by 264 BCE, the year Volsinii, the Etruscan Velzna, fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. His festival, the Vertumnalia, was held on 13 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Propertius describes a bronze statue of Vortumnus made by Mamurius Veturius, who was also credited with the twelve ritual shields, the ancilia, of the priests of Mars known as the Salii. It replaced an ancient maple statue said to have been brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>. The statue stood in a simple shrine in the Vicus Tuscus near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Forum Romanum</a> and was decorated according to the changing seasons. In Propertius's poem the statue speaks in the first person to a passer-by.</p><p>The god could change his form at will. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>'s Metamorphoses he gains entry to the orchard of Pomona by disguising himself as an old woman. He then tells the tale of Iphis and Anaxarete as a warning of the dangers of rejecting a suitor, and so wins her. The tale has been called the first exclusively <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> tale. The base of the statue was found in 1549, perhaps still in place, but has since been lost. An inscription recorded a restoration under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> in the early 4th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The story of Vertumnus and Pomona appealed to European sculptors and painters from the 16th to the 18th century, since it set youthful female beauty against an aged crone and carried a disguised erotic subtext. It was woven into tapestries, among them a mid-16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> piece in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and François Boucher supplied designs for a Gobelins series of 1775 to 1778. Camille Claudel made a marble Vertumnus and Pomona in 1905, and Joseph Brodsky wrote a poem about the god. Modern readers differ on the episode: David Littlefield finds a movement from rape to mutual desire, while Roxanne Gentilcore reads deception and veiled threat in it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vertumnus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
